WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.708
AI governance should be practical enough to support adoption.

00:00:03.708 --> 00:00:08.920
If governance is only a slogan or a long document that teams ignore,

00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:11.326
it will not protect the organization.

00:00:11.326 --> 00:00:16.538
A useful program defines approved use cases, data handling rules, model approval criteria,

00:00:16.538 --> 00:00:20.146
vendor review, access control expectations, logging requirements, evaluation standards,

00:00:20.146 --> 00:00:22.953
incident response, vulnerability management, and change control.

00:00:22.953 --> 00:00:24.556
Governance should be risk-based.

00:00:24.556 --> 00:00:29.367
A low-risk internal drafting assistant does not need the same controls as

00:00:29.367 --> 00:00:31.773
a system that uses regulated data,

00:00:31.773 --> 00:00:34.980
supports customer-facing decisions, or can trigger production workflows.

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:40.192
The right level of review depends on data sensitivity, user population, tool access,

00:00:40.192 --> 00:00:45.404
external dependencies, impact of errors, and the ability to detect and correct problems.

00:00:45.404 --> 00:00:46.607
Ownership is essential.

00:00:46.607 --> 00:00:50.616
Teams should know who approves models, who maintains retrieval collections,

00:00:50.616 --> 00:00:55.427
who reviews tool permissions, who handles incidents, who monitors cost and reliability,

00:00:55.427 --> 00:00:57.833
and who communicates changes to users.

00:00:57.833 --> 00:00:58.635
Without ownership,

00:00:58.635 --> 00:01:05.050
a private AI platform can drift into unmanaged use even when the original design was careful.

00:01:05.050 --> 00:01:08.658
Responsible rollout includes pilots, feedback loops, documented limitations, training,

00:01:08.658 --> 00:01:09.861
and continuous review.

00:01:09.861 --> 00:01:14.672
Governance should make safe paths easier, not merely slow down every request.

00:01:14.672 --> 00:01:17.478
Sovereign AI is not a one-time installation.

00:01:17.478 --> 00:01:23.091
It is a managed capability that must be maintained as models, data, vendors, infrastructure,

00:01:23.091 --> 00:01:24.695
and organizational needs change.

00:01:24.695 --> 00:01:26.699
Governance should also produce evidence.

00:01:26.699 --> 00:01:30.709
Approval records, model inventories, data-flow notes, evaluation summaries, incident records,

00:01:30.709 --> 00:01:34.317
and change histories help teams answer reasonable questions later.

00:01:34.317 --> 00:01:39.930
What changed, who approved it, what data was involved, and what controls were tested?

00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:44.340
Those records make responsible AI operations easier to defend and improve.
